  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nThe right entrance region of the upper level jet will move across \nthe area this evening, helping increase convergence along the low to \nmid level boundary. Much drier air was filtering in behind the \nsurface front, with 30 and 40 degree dew points in central SD. With \n1500-2000 J/kg CAPE ahead of this front and about 40 knots of deeper \nlayer shear, a few stronger updrafts will be possible later this \nafternoon. For now the better chances appear to be from northeast NE \ntowards Storm Lake and points south. The main severe threat should \nexit the area by early evening as cooler and drier air settles in \nbehind the exiting right entrance region.\n\nWednesday should prove to be a pleasant day with decreased humidity \nand seasonally warm temperatures in the upper 70s to lower 80s. \nNorthwest winds may gust as high as 25 mph, with lighter winds \nacross northwest IA. High pressure at the surface will drift across \nthe area tonight and allow for some cool morning lows, mainly low to \nmid 50s but a few lower spots could  see lows as cool as the upper \n40s.\n\nUpper level troughing digs across the Northern Rockies Thursday \nwhich will swing lower level winds around to the south, allowing \nfor slightly warmer temperatures, generally lower to mid 80s. \n\nAs this upper level trough spreads onto the Plains Friday, an \nenvironment that potentially supports severe weather will set up. \nThe challenge that day will be breaking the cap. If the cap can be \nbroken, 2000-3000 J/kg CAPE along with about 30-40 knots of deep \nlayer shear would support a few severe updrafts.  \n\nOnce this front moves through Friday night, much cooler air will \nsettle into the region. Highs Saturday through Monday will likely be \nin the 70s, with some 60s possible on Sunday. Lows each morning \nshould be roughly 45 to 55.\n\nSunday into Sunday night will see another fairly strong trough move \nthrough which increases warm air advection and should bring fairly \nwidespread showers and thunderstorms. For now any sever weather \nlooks to remain very isolated.",
